<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Illyricum (Roman province)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Illyricum was the Roman province on the eastern shore of the Adriatic, formed in stages during the 2nd and 1st centuries BCE as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> subdued the Illyrian peoples of the coast and hinterland. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> governed it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-illyrian-revolt">Great Illyrian Revolt</a> of 6–9 CE it was divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> in the south and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> in the north, though the name Illyricum lived on for a wider administrative region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/illyricum-roman-province.jpg" alt="Illyricum (Roman province)" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gyalu22, original by Immanuel Giel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Illyria_SPQR.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Illyricum took in the Illyrian lands in the south, along the east coast of the Adriatic Sea and its inland mountains, and the plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> in the north. The southern part stretched from the River Drin in what is now northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> to Istria and the River Sava. Pannonia lay north of the mountains, up to the westward bend of the River Danube, and included present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. As the province developed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amfissa">Salona</a>, near modern Split, became its capital.</p><p>The region was a valuable source of soldiers for the empire. Its native peoples had a reputation for fighting ability, and from the 3rd to the 6th century some of the most useful Roman troops were recruited from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>. Several emperors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crisis-of-the-third-century">Crisis of the Third Century</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-gothicus">Claudius II</a> and Aurelian, were born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amfissa">Salona</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> fought three Illyrian wars between 229 and 168 BCE. The first, in 229–228 BCE, followed attacks by Illyrian pirates on the ships of Rome's Italian allies and the growing power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardiaei">Ardiaei</a>, a tribe of what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>. The second, in 219–18 BCE, was also won by Rome. In 168 BCE the Ardiaei joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> against Rome in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-macedonian-war">Third Macedonian War</a> and were quickly defeated. In 59 BCE the lex Vatinia gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> the governorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Gallia Cisalpina</a> and Illyricum, with three legions based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a>, for five years.</p><p>Between 35 and 33 BCE Octavian campaigned in Illyricum, partly because of raids on north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and piracy in the Adriatic. Illyricum became a formal province in 27 BCE, at first under the senate, and in 11 BCE it became an imperial province under Publius Cornelius Dolabella. Rebellions in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> from 14 to 10 BCE, known as the Pannonian war, are poorly recorded, mostly in brief accounts by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a>.</p><p>The Batonian War of 6–9 CE began when native auxiliaries gathered for a campaign against the Marcomanni rebelled under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bato-the-daesitiate">Bato the Daesitiate</a>. Bato the Breucian led the Breuci of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, and the two leaders joined forces on Mount Alma. Tiberius took command of the Roman army and met their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics with counterinsurgency operations. Paul, writing to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in the winter of 57–58 CE, names Illyricum as the westernmost point of his missionary travels so far.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Illyricum (Roman province)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Daorsi were absorbed into <b>Roman Illyricum</b> by the end of the 1st century BCE, and their region later belonged to the province of Dalmatia.</blockquote><a href="/state/daorsi">Daorsi</a></li><li><blockquote>The Iapydes were subdued by 34 BCE and absorbed into the province of <b>Illyricum</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/iapydes">Iapydes</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Third Illyrian War of 168 BCE Rome captured King Gentius at Scodra and split the kingdom, which later became the province of <b>Illyricum</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/illyrian-kingdom">Illyrian Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The Heruli scattered: some moved into Roman territory and became federates in <b>Illyricum</b>, others went to Scandinavia according to Procopius.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-heruli">Kingdom of the Heruli</a></li></ul></section>
